            <ArticleTitle>Tuberculous peritonitis and tuberculous endometritis diagnosed in the same patient by high clinical suspicion and a minimally invasive approach.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1112-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Tuberculous peritonitis and tuberculous endometritis are extremely rare entities in the Western world. In this article, we discuss a rare case of a 40-year-old Nigerian female presenting with abdominal pain, distension and secondary amenorrhea, and show that a high clinical suspicion combined with minimally invasive procedures can help physicians make the diagnosis, avoid extensive surgery, and rapidly initiate appropriate therapy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
